!! Syntax

```
C4Context
    Person(alias, "Label", "Description")
    Person_Ext(alias, "Label", "Description")
    System(alias, "Label", "Description")
    System_Ext(alias, "Label", "Description")
    Boundary(alias, "Label") {
        System(inner, "Label", "Description")
    }
    Rel(from, to, "Label", "Technology")
    BiRel(from, to, "Label")
```

|!Keyword|!Shape/Meaning|
|`Person`|Blue person icon — internal user|
|`Person_Ext`|Grey person icon — external user|
|`System`|Blue box — system in scope|
|`System_Ext`|Grey box — external system|
|`SystemDb`|Blue cylinder — system with database|
|`Boundary`|Dashed boundary grouping systems|
|`C4Context`|Level 1: system context|
|`C4Container`|Level 2: containers within a system|
|`C4Component`|Level 3: components within a container|

!! Tips

* Element types (`Person`, `System`, `System_Ext`) are color-coded and labeled inline — no extra legend table is needed.
* Use `C4Context` for stakeholder-level conversations, `C4Container` to show deployment architecture, and `C4Component` for team-level design discussions.
* The fourth argument to `Rel(from, to, "Label", "Technology")` adds a technology/protocol label below the relationship arrow.
* `BiRel` creates a bidirectional arrow for symmetric interactions.
* Keep each level focused on one zoom: avoid mixing `Person` and `Container` shapes in the same diagram.
* See also: [[Architecture Diagram]] for infrastructure topology, [[Sequence Diagram]] for runtime message flows.

